<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Karen Lochhead - Full Pack Part 2</title></head><body><h1>Karen Lochhead - Full Pack Part 2</h1><p>Karen Lochhead — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 2)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>03-19 Performance and Pipeline Review</p><p>Plaud file id: 0b04e5552da97c6ca69e716b111625ed</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Performance and Pipeline Review</p><p>This is a comprehensive performance review meeting between manager Bob Duncan, advisor Karen Lochhead, and admin Megan Gordon. The discussion encompasses a review of outstanding action items, an analysis of Karen&#x27;s current lead pipeline and written business, a detailed evaluation of her annual performance against a £200k target, and the setting of future goals, with a particular emphasis on improving administrative efficiency, report completion, and fee generation to bridge income gaps.</p><p>---</p><p>## Informal Review and Professional Context</p><p>The meeting opens with informal conversation regarding former colleagues, setting a contextual backdrop for professional standards and industry trends. The group discusses Randall, a former colleague who has left a &quot;cushy little gig&quot; at Condé to open his own company. Karen speculates that he was motivated by the financial potential of self-employment, noting that many people, including Aidan McCourt (former Leeds BDM), are returning to advisory roles or going self-employed.</p><p>Bob provides a stark counter-narrative to Randall&#x27;s move, characterising him as an &quot;arrogant lying twat&quot; and revealing a serious incident of misconduct. Bob recalls having to manage one of Randall&#x27;s cases while he was on holiday, during which he discovered that Randall had committed &quot;massive mortgage fraud.&quot; Karen corroborates the severity of the situation, recalling that she reported it to Stuart Mair, who allegedly took no action, adopting an attitude of &quot;I don&#x27;t want to know unless he gets caught.&quot; This anecdote serves as a reminder of the compliance and ethical standards expected within their current team.</p><p>---</p><p>## Review of Outstanding Tasks and Lead Pipeline</p><p>Bob transitions the discussion to a formal review of the previous week&#x27;s &quot;extensive&quot; to-do list. He notes that 32 leads remained at the &#x27;created&#x27; or &#x27;appointment booked&#x27; stage and 13 Decision in Principles (DIPs) required checking. Additionally, specific product statuses for clients like &#x27;New York&#x27; and &#x27;Alexandria&#x27; needed correction. Karen admits she has not completed these tasks due to external pressures (&quot;war in Iran&quot; and general busyness), though Megan confirms she has just completed a report for a &quot;half-million job&quot; involving a client with a significant bonus (£629k) and share save scheme (£337k).</p><p>The analysis of Karen&#x27;s pipeline reveals she has 72 leads for the January-March period, which is comparable to colleagues like Roland (78) and Paul (82), though behind Craig (102). Bob highlights that Karen has a strong number of clients at the &#x27;DIP&#x27; stage (17), indicating she is effectively building her pipeline. However, a critical administrative gap is identified regarding the &#x27;appointment booked&#x27; status. Bob insists these must be updated to reflect outcomes (e.g., nurture, MPW). Megan clarifies that while appointments enter her super-admin workflow, the statuses often remain outdated if not actioned post-appointment. Bob emphasizes the need for Karen to update these statuses immediately after calls to ensure accurate tracking.</p><p>Looking ahead, Bob outlines changes to the lead distribution system. A new booking link will be implemented next week that operates on a &quot;first come, first served&quot; basis to prevent cherry-picking and alleviate pressure on Craig. There is also a discussion regarding weekend work, with a shift from Saturdays to every second Sunday being proposed, as lenders are closed on Saturdays.</p><p>---</p><p>## Analysis of Written Business and Monthly Performance</p><p>The focus shifts to Karen&#x27;s current written business. Bob notes that while she has submitted four mortgage applications this week totaling £4,100 in revenue, there is a significant amount of business pending administrative completion. Karen projects she has approximately £10,000 worth of business sitting in her pipeline for the month, comprised of roughly £4,000 in protection and several mortgage cases under £500,000.</p><p>Bob urges Karen to focus on converting this pending business to potentially challenge Paul for the top advisor spot, although he playfully acknowledges that Paul is likely to remain ahead. A bottleneck is identified in the completion of suitability reports and the adding of products to the &#x27;Midas&#x27; system. Megan confirms that while applications are done, half of the suitability list requires product additions before the business can be formally recognised. Bob issues a directive for Karen to close her other communication channels and focus exclusively on clearing this backlog by the end of the week.</p><p>---</p><p>## Annual Performance Against Targets and Future Goal Setting</p><p>Bob reviews Karen&#x27;s performance over the fiscal year against her targets. She is currently sitting at £151k, which is a significant improvement over the previous year but approximately £23k short of her initial £200k projection. Bob reassures her that despite the shortfall, her performance is strong, with a notable conversion rate of approximately 65% on leads (excluding those marked &#x27;Not Proceeded With&#x27;).</p><p>The discussion highlights a specific challenge with remortgages, where 50% of the volume turned out to be Product Transfers (PTs), which generate significantly lower income (roughly a third) compared to full remortgages. Looking forward to the next fiscal year, Bob and Karen agree to set a realistic but ambitious target of £200k. Bob plans to break this target down by lead type (remortgage, purchase, protection) to provide a clear roadmap. Karen expresses a preference for keeping her head down and working rather than focusing on high-pressure targets, but accepts the goal.</p><p>---</p><p>## Deep-Dive on Fee Generation and Client Case Management</p><p>A critical area for improvement identified is fee generation. Bob points out that the business average for fee conversion is 54%, whereas Karen is currently sitting at 31%. In comparison, top performers like Claire and Paul are achieving 80% and 79% respectively. Bob argues that bridging this gap could generate an additional £10,000 annually (approx. £2,500 per month) with no risk of clawback. Karen expresses hesitation about charging fees to existing clients or referrals but acknowledges the potential revenue.</p><p>The meeting concludes with a detailed case-by-case review to troubleshoot stuck cases:</p><p>- **Aaron Commandi:** Ongoing case; Karen is sourcing a better protection policy.</p><p>- **Alexandria:** Recently laid off but now contracting; Karen plans a Product Transfer and new protection, though income proof is an issue.</p><p>- **Hussein:** Declined by Aviva due to a GP report; Karen needs to find an alternative provider.</p><p>- **Michael Cooper:** Quoted £164/month for life insurance but refused; requires income protection despite a history of skin cancer. Megan is tasked with obtaining his GP report.</p><p>- **Lucy Muir:** Being difficult regarding protection; Megan to chase via phone.</p><p>- **Dan Smith:** Fee charged, but products not yet added to the system.</p><p>---</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>**@Karen Lochhead**</p><p>- [ ] Update statuses for the 32 leads currently at &#x27;created&#x27; or &#x27;appointment booked&#x27; stage - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Review and check the 13 outstanding DIPs - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Investigate the Halifax DIP decline that shows as &#x27;debt completed&#x27; - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Verify the payslip for the contractor client (husband) to confirm employment status - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Complete pending protection cases for Craig Arise, Stephanie Jordan, Jane Tracy, Alan Hunter, Sean Veer, and Sarah Dawood - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Complete the 4-5 outstanding suitability reports - [Friday Close of Play]</p><p>- [ ] Add pending products to the Midas system for all completed applications (including Dan Smith, Sean, and Kira) - [Friday Close of Play]</p><p>- [ ] Source alternative protection provider for Hussein following Aviva decline - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Source alternative life/income protection for Michael Cooper - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Attempt to contact Lucy Muir regarding protection; email if no answer - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Focus on increasing fee generation to target an additional £10k/year - [Next Fiscal Year]</p><p>**@Megan Gordon**</p><p>- [ ] Chase Michael Cooper&#x27;s GP report from LV/Vitality - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Chase Lucy Muir via phone regarding her protection case - [TBD]</p><p>**@Bob Duncan**</p><p>- [ ] Speak to Craig regarding lead volumes - [Today/Tomorrow]</p><p>- [ ] Speak to Maitland and Lewis regarding leads - [Next Week]</p><p>- [ ] Update the booking link to &#x27;first available&#x27; allocation - [Next Week]</p><p>- [ ] Facilitate discussion regarding the start date for Saturday/Sunday appointments - [Next Week]</p><p>- [ ] Break down the £200k target numbers by product type (remortgage, purchase, protection) for Karen - [TBD]</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p>## Internal Performance Review — Karen Lochhead  </p><p>**Content creation date:** 2026-03-19 12:16:09  </p><p>**Reviewer:** Bob Duncan  </p><p>---</p><p>## 1. Follow-Up From Previous Meeting</p><p>### Actions Previously Agreed</p><p>1. Review **32 leads** sitting at **“Created”** or **“Appointment Booked”**; confirm correct **outcome/status** (nurture/MPW/DIP/application/lost) and **who owns next follow-up** (**Karen vs Megan**).  </p><p>2. Review **13 DIPs** and correct incorrect **product/status fields** (examples referenced in discussion: **“Billy Townsend”** could be ignored; product status issues like **“New York Alexandria”** noted).  </p><p>3. After advice, **complete the report immediately** (same-day discipline).</p><p>### Completed vs Outstanding</p><p>- **Completed**</p><p>  - **Bob:** produced an updated leads/status report on **2026-03-19** (done immediately before the meeting).</p><p>- **Outstanding (repeated / carried forward)**</p><p>  - **Karen + Megan:** 32-lead review and status corrections **not completed**.</p><p>  - **Karen + Megan:** 13 DIP/status accuracy check **not completed**.</p><p>  - **Karen:** “report straight after advice” discipline **not evidenced** and was explicitly deprioritised.</p><p>### Reasons for Delay (as stated)</p><p>- Karen cited workload and external events (“war in Iran”, “everything went tits up”) as reasons for not completing CRM/admin actions.</p><p>### Ongoing Blockers</p><p>- Persistent reporting/admin backlog (“never-ending story”) impacting CRM hygiene and case progression.</p><p>### Behaviour Patterns (performance-impacting)</p><p>- **Avoidance / deprioritisation of CRM hygiene:** repeated carry-forward of the same actions.</p><p>- **Ownership drift:** reliance on Megan’s workflow without CRM statuses reflecting reality, creating unclear next-step ownership.</p><p>### Risks Created by Inaction</p><p>- **Reporting integrity risk:** “Appointment Booked” leads without outcomes distort conversion/lead quality MI.</p><p>- **Missed follow-up risk / pipeline leakage:** no recorded outcomes + no owner = drift and missed chases.</p><p>- **Operational friction:** emerging “Karen vs Megan” loop instead of one agreed rule for status updates.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Complete the **32-lead** and **13-DIP** clean-up with **next step + owner** recorded for each item.</p><p>- Reinstate the standard: **Karen updates CRM status/outcome immediately after each appointment** (same-day), with Megan exception-reporting mismatches.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Karen (Adviser)** accountable for outcomes/status/next steps.  </p><p>- **Megan (Admin)** supports execution and exception reporting.  </p><p>- **Bob (Other/Manager)** monitors completion and escalates repeat-missed actions.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Declining** — identical hygiene actions repeatedly missed, compounding CRM and pipeline risk.</p><p>---</p><p>## 2. Leads Performance Review (with CRM Discipline)</p><p>### Leads — Jan–Mar (period reviewed on screen)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **72 leads** across **January–March**.</p><p>- Statuses referenced: **17 MPW**, **3 “Keeping in Contact”**, plus multiple leads left at **“Appointment Booked”** with no outcomes recorded.</p><p>- Positive: strong movement into **fact find (“depth”)**; conversion described as moving “quite quickly” with emphasis on **DIP progression**.</p><p>- Peer volume context referenced verbally: **Paul ~82**, **Roland ~78**, **Craig 102** (Karen described as broadly “on par”).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month (if available)</p><p>- **Vs last week:** **worse** — last week’s lead clean-up remains outstanding.  </p><p>- **Vs last month:** not available (no month-by-month breakdown provided).</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- **Pipeline creation:** good progression into fact find/“depth”.</p><p>- **DIP-led progression:** DIP activity highlighted as a key driver of conversion.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Stale “Appointment Booked” statuses:** outcomes not being updated, creating uncertainty:</p><p>  - should the lead be nurture/MPW/DIP/application/lost?</p><p>  - is **Karen** or **Megan** responsible for the next chase?</p><p>- **Point-of-appointment CRM discipline gap:** Megan stated **Karen should update status at the end of the appointment** (and ensure notes/outcomes logged immediately).</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Stalled-lead risk:** un-outcomed appointments increase silent drop-offs.</p><p>- **False pipeline / false activity:** “Appointment Booked” limbo inflates live workload and hides process leakage.</p><p>- **Ownership gap risk:** unclear follow-up owner (Karen vs Megan) creates missed chases.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Apply one rule immediately:  </p><p>  - **Karen** updates **lead status + outcome + next step + owner** same-day after each appointment.  </p><p>  - **Megan** aligns admin workflow to CRM and produces an **exception list** where workflow ≠ CRM status.</p><p>- Triage “Appointment Booked” list: for each lead, record **outcome, next contact date, owner, and stage** (MPW/nurture/DIP/application/lost).</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Karen (Adviser):** outcomes/status/next steps after appointments.  </p><p>- **Megan (Admin):** workflow alignment + exception reporting.  </p><p>- **Bob (Other/Manager):** oversight of completion.</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant (with risk)** — client progression appears healthy, but CRM hygiene and ownership discipline remain a constraint.</p><p>---</p><p>### Leads — CRM Source/Ownership Integrity (Jenna Deacon records)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Multiple lead records show as **created by Jenna Deacon** (leaver), with at least one item appearing **booked but not reflected cleanly** in CRM.</p><p>- Operational change discussed: booking link to be updated to **“first available adviser”** to reduce bottleneck on Craig and prevent cherry-picking.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Concrete operational plan to improve distribution: **first-available adviser booking**.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Traceability gap:** uncertainty over ownership/creator fields and whether records are correctly staged (created vs booked).</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Misallocated leads / reporting distortion** if leaver-created records remain unowned or incorrectly staged.</p><p>- If booking link rollout is delayed, Karen’s diary may remain underfilled, impacting written/banked business.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Tidy lead records created by **Jenna Deacon**: confirm **correct status and ownership**.</p><p>- Implement booking link change to **first available adviser** (rollout ownership outside Karen).</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Karen (Adviser)** + **CRM/Admin support (Other)** for record tidy.  </p><p>- **Management/Admin lead (Other)** for booking link rollout.</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving (operationally)** — distribution fix is defined, but CRM cleanliness is currently dragging reliability.</p><p>---</p><p>### Leads — This Week (limited data)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **1 friend recommendation lead** mentioned on **2026-03-19**.</p><p>- Fee discussion referenced: **£495 vs £295**; Karen referenced charging **£295** previously and sensitivity on remortgages/own clients.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Active consideration of fee strategy and pricing depending on case context.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- No evidenced CRM steps for this lead in the discussion (no confirmed notes/tasks/next step/owner recorded).</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **MI/audit risk:** fee rationale and next steps not consistently recorded.</p><p>- **Follow-up risk:** referral lead may drift without an explicit logged next step and owner.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Ensure the referral lead is logged with **fee expectation, next step, and owner**.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Karen (Adviser)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Unknown / not measurable** — insufficient CRM evidence in the discussion.</p><p>---</p><p>## 3. “Where Are My Leads?” (only where relevant)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Lead-allocation bottleneck discussed: expectation that bookings increase once the booking link is updated to **first available adviser**, reducing overload on **Craig** and improving fair distribution.</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Operational fix identified (removes adviser cherry-picking).</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Perception and visibility issues persist while booking link and CRM tidy remain incomplete.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Diary underfill risk** for Karen if booking link change is delayed.</p><p>- **Reporting inconsistency risk** while leaver-created leads remain untidied/unowned.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Implement booking link update and confirm adoption.</p><p>- Tidy leaver-created CRM lead records to restore ownership clarity.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Other (Management/Admin lead):** booking link change.  </p><p>- **Karen + Other (CRM/Admin support):** CRM tidy.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving (pending execution)** — fix is agreed but value depends on implementation.</p><p>---</p><p>## 4. Applications In Progress (Case Progress / Status Accuracy)</p><p>### Halifax — DIP Declined but “Fact Find Completed” (Siobhan Rice context)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Case shows **“DIP declined — Halifax”** while still marked **“fact find/depth completed”**.</p><p>- Property context linked to **Siobhan Rice** (offer acceptance in progress; case active).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Case surfaced and discussed on **2026-03-19**; Karen planned to review.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Status coherence issue:** decline vs stage not reflected clearly, creating ambiguity (stop vs rework vs documentation issue).</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Criteria/document risk:** husband’s employment moved into a contractor arrangement (Rolls Royce “contract first” detail); payslip wording may change lender suitability.</p><p>- **Delay risk:** unclear status/plan can cause drift and missed chases.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Karen to check husband’s **payslip wording** and reassess lender/packaging.</p><p>- Update CRM with **Halifax decline reason**, revised plan, **next step and owner**.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Karen (Adviser)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving (micro-level)** — issue identified and assigned; depends on CRM update and execution.</p><p>---</p><p>### Maximus — Documentation Delay</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Described as “a disaster”; documents outstanding (receipt not confirmed).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Issue recognised.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Missing documents are blocking progress; no documented chase plan evidenced in the discussion.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Slippage risk** due to document delay and unclear chase ownership.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Confirm missing documents and set a chase plan.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Karen (Adviser)** (Admin support only if explicitly delegated)</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant** — blocked by missing documents with no evidenced resolution plan.</p><p>---</p><p>### Alexandra Petrie — Product Transfer (PT) due to income change</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Job loss/variable income; currently on variable rate; plan is **PT**.</p><p>- Protection desired but income proof is an issue.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- PT identified as pragmatic given current circumstances.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Protection progression constrained by inability to evidence income.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Affordability / compliance risk** if protection proceeds without adequate evidence.</p><p>- **Client outcome risk** if PT is delayed.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Proceed with PT; document income constraints and protection next steps in CRM.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Karen (Adviser)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant** — plan exists but risk remains until progressed and documented.</p><p>---</p><p>### Harriet — Re-engaged after deployment</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Was deployed (“Afghanistan or Syria”); re-engaged by email; requires cover (linked to buying land).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Re-contact initiated.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- No confirmed appointment/recommendation logged in the discussion.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Drift risk if next step is not diarised and recorded.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Confirm protection needs and proceed to recommendation; record next steps.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Karen (Adviser)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving (early)** — engagement restarted, needs conversion steps.</p><p>---</p><p>### Gurney / Daniel Barnes — Early stage (added on the 17th)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **Gurney** and **Daniel Barnes** referenced as “added… on the **17th**” (month not stated) and treated as “fine” early stage.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Cases appear captured in CRM.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Insufficient stage detail provided to validate progress or next steps.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Stage drift risk if next steps are not owned and timebound.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Ensure stage, next step, and owner are current in CRM.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Karen (Adviser)**</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Unknown** — insufficient detail on progress cadence.</p><p>---</p><p>## 5. Protection in Progress</p><p>### Current Position (named cases and issues)</p><p>- **Marty McQueen** — protection “sorted” (~**£90** referenced).  </p><p>- **Jennifer Marr** — protection removed from to-do as “sorted”.  </p><p>- **Sean Veer** — “good protection” (~**£60**).  </p><p>- **Timothy King** — still needs to be “on that” list (task pending).  </p><p>- **Rhys and Craig** — still needs to be “on that” list (task pending).  </p><p>- **Unnamed client** — “one protection… only **£30**”; Karen chasing but client not identified in discussion (tracking risk).  </p><p>- **Maisie** — explicitly stated as having **no protection**.  </p><p>- **Tina/Trina** — protection need discussed; expected premium **~£100/month**; Bob directed Megan can take over booking once details provided.  </p><p>- **Aaron Commandi** — long-running; has protection already; Karen seeking a better policy.  </p><p>- **Leslie Kiggins** — reports/nurse pathway; appointment **booked on 2026-03-18**.  </p><p>- **Hussein** — **Aviva declined** after GP report; complaint context; move to alternative provider required.  </p><p>- **Michael Cooper** — underwriting passed; life quote **£164/month** (client not taking); client wants IP; history of skin cancer (removed). Needs GP report from **LV/Vitality** and re-market.  </p><p>- **Lucy Muir** — contact attempts failing; agreed escalation: call again then email if no answer.  </p><p>- **Jia Pandey** — confirm whether Vitality supports **teachers** (eligibility/suitability query).  </p><p>- **Nithan (Aurora)** — product added but **report not done** (CRM/compliance completion gap).</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Not available.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Multiple protection items closed off on **2026-03-19** (Marty McQueen, Jennifer Marr, plus one other implied).</p><p>- Active provider switching strategy where insurer process is poor (Hussein / Aviva).</p><p>- Escalation approach agreed for non-responsive client (Lucy Muir).</p><p>- Booking/admin load can be shifted to Megan for Maisie and Tina/Trina once details are provided.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Open tasks without closure detail:** Timothy King and Rhys and Craig still pending.</p><p>- **Client identification gap:** unnamed **£30** case cannot be tracked reliably.</p><p>- **Contact failure delays:** Lucy Muir not answering; protection progress stalls without escalation completion.</p><p>- **Third-party dependency delays:** GP reports/nurse appointments (Michael Cooper, Leslie Kiggins).</p><p>- **CRM/compliance lag:** Nithan (Aurora) report outstanding despite product being added.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Protection revenue slippage** (material because protection is a major share of projected income).</p><p>- **Client risk / missed cross-sell:** Maisie has no protection; delays leave clients uncovered.</p><p>- **Complaint/service risk:** Hussein (Aviva decline + complaint context) and Lucy Muir (unresolved objection/contact failure).</p><p>- **Reporting risk:** unnamed £30 case and incomplete CRM notes reduce visibility and MI accuracy.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Update CRM/to-do with outcomes and next steps for: **Timothy King**, **Rhys and Craig**.</p><p>- Identify and update the unnamed **£30** protection case record (notes + chase task).</p><p>- **Lucy Muir:** call again; if no contact, send the agreed email escalation.</p><p>- **Hussein:** move away from Aviva; document decline reason and next provider plan in CRM.</p><p>- **Michael Cooper:** obtain GP report (LV/Vitality) and re-market for more affordable life/IP options.</p><p>- **Leslie Kiggins:** diarise and track outcome following **2026-03-18** booking.</p><p>- **Jia Pandey:** confirm Vitality eligibility for teachers and record outcome.</p><p>- **Maisie / Tina/Trina:** Karen to provide client contact details to Megan so booking can be completed; ensure ownership is clear in CRM.</p><p>- **Nithan (Aurora):** complete the outstanding report.</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Karen (Adviser):** protection progression, recommendations, CRM notes/outcomes for named cases (unless delegated).  </p><p>- **Megan (Admin):** chase GP report for **Michael Cooper**; book protection discussions for **Maisie** and **Tina/Trina** once details received.  </p><p>- **Bob (Other/Manager):** oversight/escalation on stalled protection throughput where needed.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Declining** — multiple stalled protection items driven by contact gaps, third-party report delays, and incomplete CRM/reporting, while protection is a key revenue driver.</p><p>---</p><p>## 6. Written Business – This Month (where data exists)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Written for the week referenced: **£4,100**.</p><p>- Karen estimated **~£10,000** “sitting there” to convert (fees + mortgages + protection) if outstanding work is completed.</p><p>- Karen stated she has “run” **three** cases (stage not specified).</p><p>- Wider sales position referenced: **151.56k written to date** vs **200k prior projection** (**23k short**).</p><p>- Expectation stated: **+10k minimum** this month, leaving **15k** to deliver next month.</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- Karen stated written “didn’t go up this week” as expected (no prior figure provided).</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Fee charging restarted (“fee situations again”).</p><p>- Several higher-value mortgages referenced (including “two… about **£500k**” and “there’s three”).</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Conversion/admin lag:** Midas not updated; suitability/product updates outstanding prevent conversion into written figures.</p><p>- Report backlog persists (Karen referenced **4–6 reports** still to complete; inconsistent count stated in discussion).</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Revenue slippage risk:** pipeline exists but written conversion delayed by missing Midas/suitability/report completion.</p><p>- **Next-month concentration risk:** reliance on delivering **15k** next month increases exposure if cases stall.</p><p>### Acti